{-
   Structural field requirements - `a.field: b`.

   A field is reached by *name* in the source and by *index* in a body, and a generic body is where
   the two part company: it is resolved once, so it cannot hold an index that differs per
   instantiation. What it holds instead is the **requirement**, as a projection step whose payload is
   the requirement's position - `Place::Property` - and the index arrives where the substitution
   does.

   That is the same sentence the rest of this stage already makes about a callee and about a count,
   and stating it the same way is what keeps three features one mechanism:

    - the signature says what is there, so `x.size` on an unconstrained `a` is still an error and on
      a constrained one is not;
    - the *call* proves it, against the argument list it wrote - which is why a mismatch names the
      call rather than something inside a body the author did not write;
    - the specialization spends it, and the projection that comes out is the one concrete code would
      have used. `Implementation-Generics.md` part 5 puts it as "specialization replaces the witness
      call with the same direct projection concrete code would have used".

   A body generic in `a` calling another that requires the same field is the second answer, and it is
   the same split `dischargeRequirement` makes for a class: a concrete subject has the field, and a
   subject that is still a variable is answered by what *this* signature required.
-}

data Vessel {size: I32, mark: I32}
data Crate {size: I32}

-- A subject with all three kinds of field on it: one that fills its storage, one that does not, and
-- one an erased body is asked about at a type it cannot see.
data Flagged {size: I32, flag: Bool, mark: I32}

-- The requirement declares `a` and says what it has. Nothing else in the signature mentions a field,
-- and the body would not compile without it.
fn (a.size: I32) measure(x: a) -> I32 = x.size

-- Two requirements about one subject are two components, which is what makes them disjoint.
fn (a.size: I32, a.mark: I32) label(x: a) -> I32 = x.size + x.mark

-- Passed along rather than discharged: `a` is still a variable here, so what proves the callee's
-- requirement is the one this signature states about the same variable.
fn (a.size: I32) twice(x: a) -> I32 = measure(x) + measure(x)

{-
   The same requirement in a body compiled **once**, where the field is reached through the witness
   the caller put in the property slot rather than through an offset the substitution supplied.

   The slot holds a `{read, set}` pair and not a displacement, and `erase.cpp`'s `kPropertyRead` is
   where the reason is written: a packed field has no offset, a computed one has no storage, a host
   field is a name, and an FFI layout is somebody else's rule. All four are a *how*, and a number can
   only carry a *where*. What the body does instead is Design.md's tier 1 - read the field into a
   temporary, use it, and commit it back - which is the same shape a generic mutable field access has
   to take whatever the layout underneath turns out to be.

   Both halves are exercised, and the write is the one that says the pair is real: `x.size` inside
   `bump` names storage in `main`'s frame, so a `set` that wrote into the temporary and stopped there
   would leave `v.size` at 10 and this fixture would answer -1.
-}
@generic
fn (a.size: I32) bump(&x: a) -> I32:
    x.size = x.size + 1
    return x.size

{-
   The other two shapes a property slot comes in, and what decides which - `PropertyForm`.

   A slot is one cell, and what a body does with it is read off the *declared field type* rather than
   out of the data. That is what keeps a field access from being a branch, and it is the same
   argument `isNarrowRepr` made in the old stage about which shape a `&T` takes: both ends have the
   declaration, so neither has to be told what the other chose.

    - `size: I32` fills its own storage, so the cell is a **byte offset** and the body emits the
      `add` and the load a concrete projection would have. That is `measure` and `bump` above, and it
      is what a property slot should be as often as possible.

    - `flag: Bool` owns **one bit of the byte** it was given, so it may be co-packed into a
      neighbour's the day `packBits` runs - and a reference to it therefore has to say where it
      starts. The cell carries the offset with the shift above it, and the body loads the unit,
      shifts and masks on the way out and reads-modifies-writes on the way in. The shift is zero at
      every site today because nothing here packs yet; what this pins is that the *encoding* and the
      reader are already the ones a packed field needs.

    - `mark: b` is a field whose **type the body does not know**. There is no width to load with and
      no mask to apply, so the slot holds a `{read, set}` pair instead and the body calls through it.
      Both accessors take the `simple` convention - three argument registers, and every other one
      left to the caller - which is what makes a call affordable where an `add` was the alternative.

   The last is also why the temporary in `opaqueMark` is an `alloca` over a *loaded* size: the body
   is reading a value whose width is in a descriptor, which is the same shape every other erased
   allocation takes.

   `guarded` is 6: `flag` reads false, is set, reads true, and `mark` is 7 through a witness whose
   `Peek` adds one - once directly and once through a signature that passed the requirement on.
-}
class Peek(b):
    fn peek(x: b) -> I32

instance Peek(I32):
    fn peek(x: I32) -> I32 = x + 1

@generic
fn (a.flag: Bool) readFlag(x: a) -> Bool = x.flag

@generic
fn (a.flag: Bool) setFlag(&x: a) -> Bool:
    x.flag = True
    return x.flag

{-
   The requirement **decides** `b`, which is what makes a generic field worth having.

   Nothing in `opaqueMark`'s argument list mentions `b` and no caller can write it down, so without
   the dependency this is a signature no call could satisfy. `a.field: b` states the arrow outright -
   the subject decides, the result is decided - and `fillFromProperty` spends it: at a concrete
   subject the field's own declared type is the answer, and at a subject that is still a variable the
   enclosing signature's requirement about the same field is. That is the same split
   `fillFromRequirement` makes for a class's functional dependency, and it runs in the same fixpoint
   so that `Peek(b)` beside it may be answered by what the field decided.
-}
@generic
fn (a.mark: b, Peek(b)) opaqueMark(x: a) -> I32 = peek(x.mark)

-- Passed along rather than decided: `a` is still a variable here, so what answers `c` is the
-- requirement *this* signature wrote about the same field.
fn (a.mark: b, Peek(b)) forwarded(x: a) -> I32 = opaqueMark(x)

fn guarded() -> I32:
    let &f = Flagged {size: 1 :: I32, flag: False, mark: 7 :: I32}
    if readFlag(f) then return -1
    if setFlag(f) == False then return -2
    if f.flag == False then return -3
    if opaqueMark(f) != 8 then return -4
    if forwarded(f) != 8 then return -5
    return 6

fn main() -> I32:
    let g = guarded()
    if g != 6 then return g

    let &w = Vessel {size: 10 :: I32, mark: 3 :: I32}
    let bumped = bump(w)
    if w.size != 11 then return -1
    if bumped != 11 then return -2

    let v = Vessel {size: 10 :: I32, mark: 3 :: I32}
    let c = Crate {size: 100 :: I32}
    return measure(v) + label(v) + twice(c)
